// Generated by build.mjs from news.mjs: what the page shows under Help › What’s new. Do not edit: add an entry in news.mjs.
export const NEWS = [
  ["1.1","12 October 2026",["The <b>Mirrors</b> group of the parts list has the kinds most often wanted, each the mirror begun with its settings: <b>Concave</b>, <b>Convex</b>, <b>Parabolic</b>, <b>Off-axis parabola</b> (the 90° one), <b>Partial reflector</b> and <b>Chirped mirror</b>; a <b>Dichroic mirror</b> (a filter that reflects what it blocks) and a <b>Retroreflector</b> (a right-angle prism turned to send a beam back the way it came) are listed there too.","A <b>Steering mirror</b>: a mirror can be steered by a signal, as a galvo or a piezo mirror is. Its <b>tilt</b> input takes a wire from the circuits and turns it in the bench plane by its <b>Turn</b> (° per V); photodetectors see the light as it is steered (a beam scanned across a pinhole, a pointing offset), while the bench draws the mirror at rest.","The parts list, tidier: the <b>Lamp</b> and the Circular lamp are one part, a lamp whose <b>Shines</b> setting is every way (3D) or round the bench plane only (2D, fast); the AOM, the EOM and the optical chopper have a group of their own, <b>Modulators</b>; and the fibre tip and collimator are listed under <b>Tabs</b>, with the link plane.","JEKfilm is now JEKray’s <b>Coating designer</b>, listed under JEKray beside the Optics designer. Its coatings save as <b>.coat</b> files, and a .jekfilm saved before still opens. Faces offer designed coatings as before, marked (Coating designer).","<b>JEKrf</b>, for RF and millimetre waves, is an app of its own, built on JEKray: its benches are <b>.jekrf</b> files with the RF and circuits parts, and its <b>RF designer</b> opens in its own tab, each design a <b>.rfd</b> used on JEKrf’s benches as the Optics designer’s are on JEKray’s. JEKray no longer offers the RF parts or the RF designer; a bench with RF parts still opens here. JEKrf is experimental while it is checked.","<b>Experimental features</b> are switched on and off in one place for every app: <b>Experimental features…</b> in JEKsuite’s account menu (Help › Experimental features in JEKray opens it). The apps not yet validated, JEKrf and JEKion, are among them, and JEKsuite and the site list them only while they are on.","The <b>Optics designer</b> is a program of its own, in its own tab, and each design is a file of its own, a <b>.optic</b>: saved, opened and kept in the cloud beside your benches, and listed under JEKray in JEKsuite. <b>Design…</b> on an assembly opens it there; what you change comes back to the bench as you go, as a step of Undo. <b>Use on a bench</b> puts a design on JEKray’s bench with light for it, and a bench whose design has moved on in the cloud offers the newer version.","Changing a sweep’s <b>Steps</b> (or pressing Play) on a bench that is slow to trace no longer holds the page while every step is traced first: on one bench that was 48 seconds for 30 steps, and read as a crash. The steps are seen to in the background, and a field shared by every step is worked out once, not once a step.","A sweep that leaves the beams as they are (a particle turned, a part moved off the beam) now works out every step: it was judged too large for memory by its one shared field and rendered one step in three, the rest showing a stale picture of the particle’s light. Ticking a particle’s <b>Draw an estimate of its scattered field</b> or <b>Draw its radiation pattern in 3D</b> off while a sweep plays no longer traces every step again. A paused sweep keeps what was worked out, and its slider goes to the sweep’s own steps, so pulling it shows each at once. A rod tilted by half a turn more is the same rod, so its scattering is solved once for both: a sweep right round solves half as many.","The <b>electrical feedthrough</b> has one connector, as a node has: a signal wired into it goes on from it on the same tab and from its partner on the other. It had an in and an out, and its out gave only what went into its partner, so a generator into a feedthrough and on to a scope on one tab read nothing. Benches saved with the old two connectors open as they were.","A photodiode’s oscilloscope in <b>Roll</b> follows a sweep: while any sweep plays it plots the reading against the sweeps’ own time, a point a step over the last cycle, the level and each beat’s swing as lines of their own (it stood still before, three beat periods wide). <b>Draw light by its power</b> is now <b>Fade weak rays and beams</b>, under Show the rays. <b>View › True colour field in 3D</b> draws the diffracted field in the light’s own colours, so white light through a prism fans out from red to blue. In 3D the wheel zooms towards the point the view is being turned about while the turn is held, and for a moment after. <b>How the rays look…</b> is with the experimental features for now.","Sweeps, steadier: a sweep’s <b>Steps</b>, <b>From</b> and <b>To</b> are taken when you press Enter or leave the box, not at each digit (every digit used to start its render again). A sine or triangle sweep works each position out once, not once on the way up and again on the way down, so its field renders in about half the time. After a change of Steps a field too large for memory keeps to the steps that fit. A rod’s <b>Axis tilt up</b> swept past 180° comes round (0 to 360° turns it right round). Ticking a screen’s <b>Show the diffraction pattern on its face</b> while a sweep plays no longer makes it stutter, and turning 3D on while the field is still arriving no longer leaves every later field to be worked out on the page.","A sweep with Steps blank no longer takes a new value at every frame: it plays on an automatic fixed set of steps, about 30 a second at its rate (from 48 up to 600 a cycle, shown in the Steps box as <i>auto</i>), the same points its field is rendered at. Each cycle comes back to the same values, so every step is traced, lit and its Particle solved once and then reused, its scattered light added all at once like the field, and the rays always match the field. Steps typed in now go up to 600.","A sweep follows the bench: the <b>Render the diffracted field</b> tick is gone. With the diffracted field shown (Light), Play starts the sweep at once with the parts, rays, beams’ outlines and readings, works the field out for its steps in the background under its bar, and adds it to every step at once when it is ready; a Particle’s scattered light comes the same way, each when it is ready, so no step shows a layer another lacks. With the field off, none is worked out. The 30-second hold, the bench frozen while a long render went on, is gone where the sweep can play beside it. A bench saved with the tick opens with its own Light setting deciding.","A bench opens in <b>2D</b> again; the 2D / 3D switch at the bench’s bottom right turns 3D on, and a tab keeps the view chosen for the visit.","Turning the 3D view while a sweep plays its rendered field no longer stutters at each step. A sweep that comes back to a value (a triangle sweep of a rod’s tilt, on its way up and down) had its light, the particle’s scattered glow among it, built again at every step of every cycle; now each step’s light is built once and then shown as it is. A cavity on the bench with no light to settle in is no longer solved again at every step, and the scattered light past the parts is built faster.","A <b>Particle</b> can draw its radiation pattern in 3D: tick <b>Draw its radiation pattern in 3D</b> under On the bench on its Scattering tab. A see-through surface about the particle reaches out in each direction as far as it scatters that way, on a linear scale whatever the Scale, so a small sphere’s doughnut and a rod’s lobes can be seen, turning with the particle and following a sweep. Off unless asked for.","A sweep held while its steps are worked out no longer leaves the bench stretched or blank when a panel opens or closes beside it, in 2D or 3D.","A <b>Particle</b> made a rod or a spheroid with Model set to Mie now uses the dipole array, so its scattering follows its shape and how it is turned (Mie holds for a sphere only); the Scattering tab says so.","The page stays free while the light catches up. A sweep that renders its field on a bench slow to trace (fibres, a Particle, many parts) takes each step’s trace from the worker that rendered it or solved its particle, so its first cycle plays without tracing every step on the page (on a bench with fibres and a Particle, a wait of a fifth of a second at each step); a Particle’s answer comes with the bench traced with it, so the page does not trace it again as the answer arrives. Every such trace is checked against a fresh one on the examples. A receiving horn’s coupling is worked out faster, to the same numbers. The 3D view works out the field’s glow at half the size each way while it turns, pans or zooms and while a sweep plays, and at full size again once it is still. A sweep whose rendered field would not fit in memory (the levitated-trap example: 240 steps of 140 MB) renders every so many steps rather than closing the tab."],["<b>Export data</b>: the bench’s readouts, plots, screens’ rays and fields, beams, sweeps and parts as CSV or JSON (a free account), or Excel, NumPy and MATLAB files (JEKrayPro); <code>save()</code> in Scripts.","<b>Exports with a free account</b>: pictures, plots, data, .zmx and CAD files; a .jekray file needs none. <b>Free account</b> and <b>Pro</b> badges mark what needs what.","A <b>3D view</b> of every bench, a switch away: every free-space part placed, tipped and rolled in all six degrees of freedom, with rays, Gaussian beams and the diffracted field followed exactly through it, misaligned cavities included.","<b>STL objects</b> from CAD on the bench (mounts, housings, baffles), saved with it and kept in My parts.","<b>Electronics</b> as a standard part of the tool, with <b>feedthroughs</b> carrying signals between tabs, and photonic chips wired to them.","<b>JEKrayPro</b> in open beta: benches in the cloud with their history, My benches and My parts, and the designers’ design work (the optimiser, tolerancing, reports, drawings and export); and the <b>documentation</b>, with the equations behind every model.","<b>Draw</b> on the bench for figures, and export just the view you have framed.","Smoother all round: sweeps that render the field play every step, parts drag smoothly in 3D, and long sums run in the background."],289],
  ["1.0","28 September 2026",["The free edition. An <b>examples gallery</b> with a picture of each bench, and a search.","<b>Guided tours</b>: focus a laser beam, and build a Michelson interferometer, step by step on a tab of their own.","<b>Earlier versions</b> (Open ▾): this browser keeps copies of your project as you work, and before you open something else.","<b>Embed</b> a live bench in a web page or course notes, from the Share link dialog.","A shared link opens with a bar to keep the bench, or go straight back to your own work.","A <b>Cite</b> button, and a licence page.","Short pulses, nonlinear optics, vapour cells and optical traps are no longer beta: each is now checked on the validation page. Polarisation spectroscopy, optical lattices and the gas damping of levitated particles stay in beta until they are checked too.","An <b>Experimental features</b> switch (under the toolbar): new features arrive there, off until you turn them on, before they reach beta.","<b>Photonic circuits</b> (experimental): grating couplers, directional couplers, MMIs, rings, phase shifters and on-chip photodiodes, joined by waveguides on a green palette, with a spectrum view; TE near 1550 nm, checked on the validation page against closed forms and SAX.","A <b>validation page</b> that runs JEKray against published results and independent codes in your browser. Vapour cells now use Alcock et al.’s vapour pressures, which put about 2.5% fewer atoms in a rubidium cell at 50 °C than before."],null,10],
  ["Before 1.0","23–27 September 2026",["Beams split where they cross an element’s edge: the part that misses carries on.","Aberration analysis across the field: Seidel and Zernike terms, spot diagrams, field curvature and distortion, with the chief ray aimed through the stop.","Nonlinear optics (beta): frequency doubling in PPLN and PPKTP, self-phase modulation and solitons in fibre.","Atoms and traps (beta): rubidium, caesium and potassium vapour cells, saturated absorption, Zeeman shifts (DAVLL, Faraday filters, polarisation spectroscopy), dipole traps, lattices, tweezers and levitated particles.","Short pulses (beta): spectral phase through glass, prisms, gratings, chirped mirrors and fibre; compressors and stretchers.","Lasers that settle at their output power; fibre subsystems with PM and multimode fibre and amplifiers.","Higher-order modes (Hermite–Gauss, Laguerre–Gauss, vortices) and SLM masks.","An optimiser over several parameters and goals, and tolerancing.","Tabs of benches joined by link planes; share links; an AI assistant guide; background tracing for big benches."],null,9]
];
